**Bounded source reconstruction:** [Vector Calculus — David Tong](../../physics/electromagnetism/sources/Vector-Calculus-Tong.lingua#r_2YD2PDK99N62GBWKXXCBY69PP5), chapter 4.1, printed pages 68–69 (chapter PDF pages 2–3), uses an upper hemisphere of radius R and F=(0,0,z+R), closed by its bottom disk. Recalculate the curved flux 5πR³/3 and disk flux −πR³, giving 2πR³/3, equal to the volume integral of divergence 1. The program stores coefficients of π exactly, not rounded π. For changed F=(0,0,az+b), a horizontal spherical band gives curved flux 2π∫₀ᴿ z(az+b)dz; the disk gives −bπR². At R=2,a=3,b=−1 these are 12π and 4π, totaling 16π. This reconstructs one mathematical example and its original extension; it is not a reproduction of observed data.

Include three failed rivals. Reversing only the bottom-disk normal gives the wrong closed-boundary answer. Using det=−3 instead of |det| for the linear-coordinate scalar area gives a negative area. Applying the zero-divergence formula to a ball containing the singular origin ignores its hypotheses. The last example requires the analytic domain argument; the program's polynomial samples do not test a singular field. Also demonstrate why one zero global residual does not establish local conservation.

The program's three-point rule is exact only for polynomial integrands of degree at most three in each independently integrated variable. On [0,1], its weights (1,4,1)/6 integrate 1,t,t²,t³ to 1,1/2,1/3,1/4. Linearity and affine substitution prove the rule for that class and tensor products of it. The box faces, graph pullbacks and hemisphere band expressions meet the bound. For t⁴ the rule gives 5/24 instead of 1/5; it is not a general exact integration API. There is no numerical-error estimate for unrelated integrands.

